# Naming and Single-Responsibility Audit: Reconciled Consensus, Findings
File: openOODA/audits/naming/consensus.oot

Provenance: converted from `openOODA/audits/naming-audit-consensus.md` (256 lines). Part 1 of 2.

Redaction note: fleet host names and tailnet addresses are written as generic
placeholders. The audited tree still holds the literals.

# Naming & Single-Responsibility Audit — Reconciled Consensus

**Reconciler:** Devin `wR:p2` (swe-2-max) — third pass, independent verification
**Date:** 2026-11-05
**Inputs:**
- `naming-audit-mcode.md` (mcode / MiniMax-M3.1-Flash, `w3:p1` — 788 lines; token-collision matrix method) — cited as **M§n**
- `naming-audit-devin.md` (Devin `wR:p1` — 283 lines; physical-subtree + RFC-conformance method) — cited as **D§n**

**Method:** Both audits were independent and read-only (no compiler, build, test, or binary executed). This third pass
re-verified **every contested, load-bearing, or divergent claim directly against the tree** — persona dispatch sources,
Makefiles, `catalog/index`, RFC 0012 §2.3 (`rfcs/0012:139-145`), `northstar.oot`, and directory inventories. Claims that failed
verification are listed in §5 **[ERRATA]**. A prior draft of this file written by `wR:p1` was superseded by this one after its
`oohealthd` RFC misread and two inherited errata were caught (§5).

**Conventions:** repo paths abbreviated to `org/name` (e.g. `apps/ooflash` = `openOODA-apps/ooflash`).

---

## 0. Bottom line

Two independent auditors, different models, different extraction methods, converged on one root cause:

> **The fleet does not have a bad-names problem — it has a name-ownership problem.** Repo dirs ↔ `BIN` names are nearly
> perfectly consistent; the defects are persona/repo tokens with **two owners or none**, absorptions declared but never enacted,
> canonical names existing only on paper, and at least four pairs of repos claiming *each other*.

The single most consequential verified fact: **21 signed, publicly installable `catalog/index` package names are simultaneously
persona tokens of other binaries** — the install surface and the runtime surface disagree about who owns a name.

- **16 of 20** top findings are shared or mutually reinforcing (§1–2)
- **11** verdict-level divergences adjudicated against the tree + RFC (§4)
- Each auditor caught serious defects the other missed entirely (§3) — the audits are complementary, not redundant
- Action plan: **5 ownership decisions → ~23 zero-risk archives → 21 catalog withdrawals → ~14 renames → 6 splits → substrate
  dedup → registry/gates** (§7)

